1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,820
Two cooling towers rise from the wooded

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:04,640
hills of Western Washington.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:07,100
They look like a matched pair.

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:09,060
Their histories are not.

5
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,740
One belonged to Washington Nuclear Project 5.

6
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,280
That project was about 14% complete

7
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,160
when it was terminated in 1982.

8
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,600
The other served Washington Nuclear Project

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:23,360
3.

10
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,960
That project reached 74% physical

11
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:30,060
completion and remained preserved for possible

12
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:32,420
completion until 1994.

13
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,160
Neither project produced commercial electricity.

14
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,900
The site was never fueled.

15
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:41,700
So the mystery is not simply why

16
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:42,960
Satsop was unfinished.

17
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:46,060
It is why two reactor projects built

18
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:48,800
side by side died 12 years

19
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:49,420
apart.

20
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:53,140
And why 74% complete was

21
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:54,920
still too expensive to finish.

22
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:57,940
On January 22nd, 1982,

23
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:01,660
the Washington Public Power Supply System terminated

24
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:04,300
Nuclear Projects 4 and 5.

25
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:06,960
Project 4 stood at Hanford.

26
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,120
Project 5 stood here at Satsop.

27
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,500
Financial advisors had reached a blunt conclusion.

28
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:16,660
The bond market could not absorb the

29
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:19,560
financing needed to carry all five nuclear

30
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:22,520
projects then being pursued by the supply

31
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,100
system.

32
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,720
At termination,

33
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:27,920
projects 4 and 5 had sold 2

34
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:30,480
.25 billion dollars in bonds.

35
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,260
Their estimated combined completion cost had climbed

36
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:36,680
to roughly 12 billion dollars.

37
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:39,800
About 8.5 billion above the original

38
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:40,900
combined estimate.

39
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,040
Project 5 itself was only about 14

40
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,040
% complete.

41
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:47,760
The physical project advanced.

42
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,840
The estimate for completing projects 4 and

43
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:53,400
5 had already moved far beyond the

44
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,060
original combined plan.

45
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:57,320
But this was not just a construction

46
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:57,940
problem,

47
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,100
it was a promise to pay.

48
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:02,480
To see how the region reached that

49
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,300
point, rewind 16 years.

50
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:08,880
Through the mid 20th century, federal hydroelectric

51
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:11,620
projects supplied most of the Pacific North

52
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:12,640
West's electricity.

53
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:14,800
By 1966,

54
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:18,180
planners projected demand growing beyond what that

55
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,380
system could provide.

56
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:23,300
Utilities in Bonneville organized a regional planning

57
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:23,760
effort.

58
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:25,500
In 1969,

59
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:28,080
that effort adopted a plan calling for

60
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:30,260
several large thermal plants.

61
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,400
The supply system moved from a small

62
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,380
organization into a builder of 5

63
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:37,580
nuclear projects.

64
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:39,540
Before 1968,

65
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,280
it had fewer than 100 employees.

66
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:44,740
By March 1979,

67
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,360
it had 1,471.

68
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,840
This was not one isolated plant running

69
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:52,620
over budget.

70
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:56,180
It was an entire portfolio competing for

71
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:56,620
engineering,

72
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:57,600
contractors,

73
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:58,440
equipment,

74
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,040
regulatory attention,

75
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,960
and financing at the same time.

76
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,540
Only one of those 5 projects would

77
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:08,500
eventually operate. Project 2 at

78
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:08,940
Hanford,

79
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:11,920
which entered commercial service in December

80
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,020
1984.

81
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,500
By July 1976,

82
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:19,140
88 customers of the Bonneville Power Administration

83
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:22,220
and one private utility had become

84
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:25,200
participants in projects 4 and 5.

85
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:28,360
The bonds were backed by contracts containing

86
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:30,460
a take or pay obligation.

87
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,800
The phrase sounds obscure.

88
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:35,340
Its meaning was painfully clear.

89
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:38,400
Participants were required to pay their share

90
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:40,020
whether the plants were completed,

91
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:41,920
operable, or operating.

92
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,180
When the projects were alive, that promise

93
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:47,540
helped make their bonds marketable.

94
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:49,580
Once construction ended,

95
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:51,920
it meant utilities could still face the

96
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:54,140
cost of plants that would never deliver

97
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:54,780
power.

98
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:58,480
GAO reported that rate increases would be

99
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:00,500
required to service the debt.

100
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:03,560
Utilities and ratepayers went to court.

101
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,160
The question was no longer only whether

102
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,480
the project should continue,

103
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,120
it was who would carry the bill

104
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:11,360
after they stopped.

105
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:13,240
On July 22,

106
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:15,000
1983,

107
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,680
the project 4 and 5 bonds went

108
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:18,580
into default.

109
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:21,900
That is the famous the supply system

110
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:23,380
financial collapse.

111
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:26,660
And yet, the neighboring project at Satsop

112
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:28,500
did not end with it.

113
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:31,760
To understand why, the two towers have

114
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:33,500
to be separated financially.

115
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,540
Project 5 belonged to the participant financed

116
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:40,100
Project 4 and 5 system.

117
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:43,320
Project 3 belonged to an earlier group

118
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:46,000
supported through net billing with Bonneville.

119
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,040
The federal agency that marketed power from

120
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:51,920
the North West's federal hydroelectric system.

121
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:55,440
Public utilities held capability shares in Project

122
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:57,780
3 and assigned them to Bonneville.

123
00:04:58,520 --> 00:04:59,100
In exchange,

124
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:02,440
Bonneville credited Project costs through their

125
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:03,920
wholesale power bills.

126
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:07,780
70% of Project 3 used that

127
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:09,540
public net billing structure.

128
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,300
Four investor owned utilities held the remaining

129
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,020
30%. A later settlement

130
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,780
changed Bonneville's exposure and

131
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:21,720
Bonneville ultimately paid all Project 3

132
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:23,060
Project costs.

133
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:26,680
The structures looked similar because both projects

134
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:28,340
were planned as large,

135
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,240
pressurized water reactors of roughly

136
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:34,200
1240 megawatts.

137
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,740
But a cooling tower cannot show who

138
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:39,120
carries the next billion dollars of risk.

139
00:05:39,331 --> 00:05:41,631
Nor can the tower show how much

140
00:05:41,631 --> 00:05:43,691
remained outside it.

141
00:05:44,011 --> 00:05:46,091
Each proposed Satsop unit,

142
00:05:46,671 --> 00:05:49,731
included a reactor system, auxiliary building,

143
00:05:50,311 --> 00:05:51,311
turbine generator,

144
00:05:51,971 --> 00:05:52,751
cooling system,

145
00:05:53,291 --> 00:05:54,211
transmission connections,

146
00:05:54,851 --> 00:05:56,211
and supporting facilities.

147
00:05:57,071 --> 00:05:59,211
The tower was the most visible piece,

148
00:05:59,471 --> 00:06:01,391
but it was only the heat rejection

149
00:06:01,391 --> 00:06:02,611
end of the plant.

150
00:06:03,331 --> 00:06:03,991
In operation,

151
00:06:04,591 --> 00:06:06,791
reactor heat would produce steam.

152
00:06:06,791 --> 00:06:09,711
The steam would spin a turbine generator,

153
00:06:10,251 --> 00:06:12,371
and the cooling system would remove waste

154
00:06:12,371 --> 00:06:15,211
heat, so water could circulate again.

155
00:06:16,051 --> 00:06:18,671
At Satsop, that chain was never completed

156
00:06:18,671 --> 00:06:20,391
and never entered service.

157
00:06:21,571 --> 00:06:24,651
Project 5 hit its financing wall first.

158
00:06:25,271 --> 00:06:27,511
Project 3 had enough support to enter

159
00:06:27,511 --> 00:06:28,591
a different state,

160
00:06:29,051 --> 00:06:30,931
neither advancing nor dead.

161
00:06:31,991 --> 00:06:34,711
Construction on Project 3 was suspended in

162
00:06:34,711 --> 00:06:36,031
July 1983.

163
00:06:36,791 --> 00:06:39,391
Suspended did not mean terminated.

164
00:06:40,311 --> 00:06:42,911
Buildings and installed systems were protected.

165
00:06:43,811 --> 00:06:46,471
Equipment and construction materials retained

166
00:06:46,471 --> 00:06:47,811
possible value.

167
00:06:48,631 --> 00:06:51,131
The option to finish the project remained

168
00:06:51,131 --> 00:06:51,991
open.

169
00:06:52,731 --> 00:06:54,231
Preservation bought time,

170
00:06:54,751 --> 00:06:56,391
it also cost money.

171
00:06:57,211 --> 00:06:59,351
Every year changed the calculation.

172
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:01,351
Equipment aged,

173
00:07:01,851 --> 00:07:04,131
current regulatory requirements moved.

174
00:07:04,131 --> 00:07:06,651
Alternative generating technology

175
00:07:06,651 --> 00:07:07,611
improved.

176
00:07:08,271 --> 00:07:11,071
The region's demand forecasts changed.

177
00:07:11,811 --> 00:07:14,731
The site therefore required work without producing

178
00:07:14,731 --> 00:07:15,491
electricity.

179
00:07:16,671 --> 00:07:19,451
Preservation meant inspecting what had been built

180
00:07:19,451 --> 00:07:22,191
and protecting material that might still be

181
00:07:22,191 --> 00:07:22,831
used.

182
00:07:23,551 --> 00:07:25,891
It also meant maintaining the option to

183
00:07:25,891 --> 00:07:28,691
restart and studying whether that

184
00:07:28,691 --> 00:07:30,451
option remained valuable.

185
00:07:31,121 --> 00:07:33,171
The longer preservation continued,

186
00:07:33,851 --> 00:07:36,111
the more the project faced a paradox.

187
00:07:37,031 --> 00:07:40,071
Keeping the option alive required spending money.

188
00:07:40,451 --> 00:07:42,731
But spending money to preserve an option

189
00:07:42,731 --> 00:07:45,331
did not prove that exercising the option

190
00:07:45,331 --> 00:07:46,451
would make sense.

191
00:07:47,471 --> 00:07:48,551
By 1994,

192
00:07:49,211 --> 00:07:52,011
Bonneville described the project 3 project as

193
00:07:52,011 --> 00:07:54,691
74% physically complete.

194
00:07:55,611 --> 00:07:58,351
Approximately 2.6 billion dollars had been

195
00:07:58,351 --> 00:08:00,971
invested. Measured in 1993

196
00:08:00,971 --> 00:08:04,071
dollars and including the investor owned utilities

197
00:08:04,071 --> 00:08:04,891
share.

198
00:08:05,671 --> 00:08:08,071
The cooling tower stood almost 500 feet

199
00:08:08,071 --> 00:08:08,631
high.

200
00:08:09,111 --> 00:08:11,851
The reactor and turbine buildings existed.

201
00:08:12,591 --> 00:08:14,511
From the road, the project could look

202
00:08:14,511 --> 00:08:15,411
close to finished.

203
00:08:16,191 --> 00:08:18,431
But completion is not measured by how

204
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:19,891
much concrete is visible.

205
00:08:20,451 --> 00:08:22,491
It is measured by what still has

206
00:08:22,491 --> 00:08:24,631
to be spent and how long that

207
00:08:24,631 --> 00:08:26,471
money remains at risk.

208
00:08:27,111 --> 00:08:29,511
Most of all, it is measured by

209
00:08:29,511 --> 00:08:32,151
whether the electricity will be needed when

210
00:08:32,151 --> 00:08:33,831
the plant finally opens.

211
00:08:34,851 --> 00:08:36,671
In June 1993,

212
00:08:37,411 --> 00:08:39,771
Bonneville began another study of whether project

213
00:08:39,771 --> 00:08:42,631
3 should remain preserved for nuclear

214
00:08:42,631 --> 00:08:43,371
completion.

215
00:08:44,071 --> 00:08:46,451
The conclusions went before the supply System's

216
00:08:46,451 --> 00:08:48,571
executive board in November

217
00:08:48,571 --> 00:08:49,671
1993

218
00:08:50,391 --> 00:08:52,911
and its board of directors in January

219
00:08:52,911 --> 00:08:54,091
1994.

220
00:08:54,621 --> 00:08:57,151
The analysis asked for practical

221
00:08:57,151 --> 00:08:57,931
questions.

222
00:08:58,791 --> 00:09:01,271
Would completion damage Bonneville's

223
00:09:01,271 --> 00:09:02,111
competitiveness?

224
00:09:02,971 --> 00:09:04,631
Did the region need the power?

225
00:09:05,411 --> 00:09:08,031
How did project 3 compare with available

226
00:09:08,031 --> 00:09:08,891
alternatives?

227
00:09:09,831 --> 00:09:12,431
And what risks came with each choice?

228
00:09:13,191 --> 00:09:15,431
This is the human scale centre of

229
00:09:15,431 --> 00:09:16,051
the story.

230
00:09:16,751 --> 00:09:18,691
The project was no longer an abstract

231
00:09:18,691 --> 00:09:19,251
monument.

232
00:09:19,771 --> 00:09:22,771
Its remaining cost would flow through institutions

233
00:09:22,771 --> 00:09:25,951
to utilities and eventually to the people

234
00:09:25,951 --> 00:09:28,651
paying for electricity across the region.

235
00:09:29,571 --> 00:09:31,731
Bonneville was already under pressure to control

236
00:09:31,731 --> 00:09:32,431
its rates.

237
00:09:33,091 --> 00:09:35,631
Its customers were considering other sources of

238
00:09:35,631 --> 00:09:38,471
power and some indicated that they might

239
00:09:38,471 --> 00:09:41,171
pay a premium for greater independence.

240
00:09:42,131 --> 00:09:45,211
Completing project 3 could push Bonneville's costs

241
00:09:45,211 --> 00:09:48,191
upward. At the same moment its customers

242
00:09:48,191 --> 00:09:49,951
were gaining alternatives.

243
00:09:50,911 --> 00:09:53,271
That is how a decision inside a

244
00:09:53,271 --> 00:09:55,931
federal power agency reaches a household

245
00:09:55,931 --> 00:09:57,391
or a small business.

246
00:09:58,171 --> 00:09:59,751
Not as a cooling tower,

247
00:10:00,471 --> 00:10:01,171
as a rate.

248
00:10:01,951 --> 00:10:03,971
The past 2.6 billion dollars could

249
00:10:03,971 --> 00:10:05,431
not answer those questions.

250
00:10:06,271 --> 00:10:08,391
That money was already gone.

251
00:10:09,331 --> 00:10:11,611
Only the next commitment could still be

252
00:10:11,611 --> 00:10:12,211
changed.

253
00:10:13,011 --> 00:10:16,151
Bonneville compared project 3 with natural gas

254
00:10:16,151 --> 00:10:17,851
combined cycle generation.

255
00:10:18,691 --> 00:10:21,691
Plants using gas and steam turbines together.

256
00:10:22,451 --> 00:10:25,631
The 1994 exhibits modeled project 3

257
00:10:25,631 --> 00:10:28,151
at 1240 megawatts.

258
00:10:29,031 --> 00:10:32,011
The combined cycle reference was 240

259
00:10:32,011 --> 00:10:32,831
megawatts.

260
00:10:33,551 --> 00:10:34,951
That difference mattered.

261
00:10:35,871 --> 00:10:38,471
Combined cycle resources could be built in

262
00:10:38,471 --> 00:10:41,471
smaller increments, letting Bonneville match new

263
00:10:41,471 --> 00:10:44,351
supply more closely to actual load

264
00:10:44,351 --> 00:10:45,011
growth.

265
00:10:46,151 --> 00:10:49,111
Bonneville's assumptions also gave the combined cycle

266
00:10:49,111 --> 00:10:52,171
alternative a shorter construction lead time.

267
00:10:53,111 --> 00:10:55,671
Three years compared with five years for

268
00:10:55,671 --> 00:10:58,331
restarting and completing project 3.

269
00:10:59,031 --> 00:11:01,651
The comparison was not one giant replacement

270
00:11:01,651 --> 00:11:04,331
plant against one giant nuclear

271
00:11:04,331 --> 00:11:04,951
plant.

272
00:11:05,591 --> 00:11:08,231
Bonneville could add combined cycle capacity

273
00:11:08,231 --> 00:11:10,851
in smaller units as demand

274
00:11:10,851 --> 00:11:11,611
appeared.

275
00:11:12,511 --> 00:11:14,811
The agency could shape and dispatch that

276
00:11:14,811 --> 00:11:17,991
output more flexibly around a hydroelectric

277
00:11:17,991 --> 00:11:21,031
system whose generation changed with

278
00:11:21,031 --> 00:11:22,011
water conditions.

279
00:11:23,071 --> 00:11:25,351
More of a combined cycle plant's costs

280
00:11:25,351 --> 00:11:28,391
were variable and avoidable when it

281
00:11:28,391 --> 00:11:30,031
was not producing power.

282
00:11:30,031 --> 00:11:33,171
Project 3 carried a larger share

283
00:11:33,171 --> 00:11:35,851
of fixed costs that Bonneville would owe

284
00:11:35,851 --> 00:11:37,051
regardless of production.

285
00:11:37,208 --> 00:11:39,328
The gas option had risks,

286
00:11:39,908 --> 00:11:42,668
including fuel prices and possible carbon

287
00:11:42,668 --> 00:11:43,328
costs.

288
00:11:43,888 --> 00:11:45,848
Bonneville recorded those two.

289
00:11:46,548 --> 00:11:49,308
This matters because the 1994 decision

290
00:11:49,308 --> 00:11:52,228
was not a claim that combined cycle

291
00:11:52,228 --> 00:11:55,348
power was risk -free, or that nuclear

